{"story": "Myth: In the wind-kissed town of Hollowcurrent, every doorway holds a folded bird of clay, a tiny guardian of shared time. At dawn, a resident presses an open palm to the clay bird, whispering a moment they will offer—a meal, a ride, a listening ear. By dusk, the birds unfold amber wings and drift to neighboring doors, tracing a quiet map of generosity.

Prosperity glides like a current: a baker shares warm loaves to ease a late shift; a carpenter trains a neighbor’s apprentice; a student tutors after dusk. The town learns to read the birds’ path: where wings gather, help travels; where a beak rests, trust rests too. Small acts become a living network, threads of heat along cold doors, stitching a shared evening into something sturdier.

Betrayal arrives as a storm that shatters a clay guardian, scattering tokens to the wind and dimming the town’s corridor. Yet forgiveness can remold the clay: gather pieces, recenter the flock, and shape a stronger guardian. Hollowcurrent learns that real wealth is not hoarded but rewoven: every offered moment a seed, every received kindness a wingbeat that lifts the next day."}

